{"slug":"workshop-library-search","kind":"workshop","title":"Library Search","fetchedAt":"2026-10-16T02:56:29.237Z","items":[{"kind":"challenge","id":"6a1c40f36755ecfb7342d18b","title":"Step 1","challengeType":0,"demoType":null,"description":"Welcome to the Library Book Search workshop! In this project, you will build a book title search by handling errors gracefully using `try`, `catch`, `throw`, and `finally`.\n\nYou have been provided with an array of available books called `library`, as well as some basic DOM variables to handle user input.\n\nIf you look at the bottom of the `script.js` file, there is an event listener attached to `searchBtn`. When clicked, it expects to call a function named `findBook`, passing in the user's input.\n\nTo get started, declare an empty function named `findBook`. This function should take a single parameter named `title`.","hints":[{"text":"You should declare an empty function named `findBook` that takes `title` as a parameter. You can use a standard function, an arrow function, or a function expression.","test":"const explorer = await __helpers.Explorer(code);\nconst { findBook } = explorer.allFunctions;\nassert.exists(findBook);\nassert.lengthOf(findBook.parameters, 1);\nassert.strictEqual(findBook.parameters[0].toString(), \"title\");"}],"seed":"<!DOCTYPE html>\n<html lang=\"en\">\n  <head>\n    <meta charset=\"UTF-8\" />\n    <meta name=\"viewport\" content=\"width=device-width, initial-scale=1.0\" />\n    <title>Library Book Search</title>\n    <link rel=\"stylesheet\" href=\"styles.css\" />\n  </head>\n  <body>\n    <div>\n      <h1>Find book title</h1>\n      <input type=\"text\" id=\"bookTitleInput\" placeholder=\"Enter book title\">\n      <button id=\"search\">Search</button>\n      <p id=\"statusMessage\"></p>\n      <p id=\"scanStatus\"></p>\n    </div>\n    <script src=\"script.js\"></script>\n  </body>\n</html>\n<style class=\"fcc-injected-styles\">\nbody {\n  display: flex;\n  justify-content: center;\n  align-items: center;\n  height: 100vh;\n}\n\ninput {\n  padding: 0.5rem;\n  width: 200px;\n}\n\nbutton {\n  padding: 0.5rem 1rem;\n  cursor: pointer;\n  background-color: #000000;\n  color: white;\n}\n\n#statusMessage {\n  margin-top: 1.5rem;\n  font-weight: bold;\n  height: 1.5rem;\n}\n</style>\n<script>\nconst library = [\n  \"The Hobbit\", \"1984\", \"Dune\", \"Fahrenheit 451\", \"To Kill a Mockingbird\",\n  \"The Great Gatsby\", \"Moby Dick\", \"Pride and Prejudice\", \"Frankenstein\"\n];\n\nconst searchBtn = document.getElementById(\"search\");\nconst statusDisplay = document.getElementById(\"statusMessage\");\nconst bookInput = document.getElementById(\"bookTitleInput\");\n\n\n\nsearchBtn.addEventListener(\"click\", () => {\n  const result = findBook(bookInput.value.trim());\n  statusDisplay.textContent = result;\n});\n</script>","seedLang":"html","region":[55,56],"solution":null,"solutionLang":null,"beforeAll":"","beforeEach":""},{"kind":"challenge","id":"6a1c45e3d23c5c908748b790","title":"Step 2","challengeType":0,"demoType":null,"description":"Now that your `findBook` function is set up, you can create the \"happy path\". This is the expected behavior of the code when everything goes right.\n\nWhen a user searches for a book, the function should return a success message. This message will eventually be displayed on the screen.\n\nInside your `findBook` function, use the `return` keyword to return `title` followed by the string `\" is available to borrow!\"`.\n\nYou can use standard string concatenation or template literals.","hints":[{"text":"Your function should use the `return` keyword to return the correct success string using string concatenation or template literals.","test":"assert.strictEqual(findBook(\"The Hobbit\"), \"The Hobbit is available to borrow!\");\nassert.strictEqual(findBook(\"Dune\"), \"Dune is available to borrow!\");"}],"seed":"<!DOCTYPE html>\n<html lang=\"en\">\n  <head>\n    <meta charset=\"UTF-8\" />\n    <meta name=\"viewport\" content=\"width=device-width, initial-scale=1.0\" />\n    <title>Library Book Search</title>\n    <link rel=\"stylesheet\" href=\"styles.css\" />\n  </head>\n  <body>\n    <div>\n      <h1>Find book title</h1>\n      <input type=\"text\" id=\"bookTitleInput\" placeholder=\"Enter book title\">\n      <button id=\"search\">Search</button>\n      <p id=\"statusMessage\"></p>\n      <p id=\"scanStatus\"></p>\n    </div>\n    <script src=\"script.js\"></script>\n  </body>\n</html>\n<style class=\"fcc-injected-styles\">\nbody {\n  display: flex;\n  justify-content: center;\n  align-items: center;\n  height: 100vh;\n}\n\ninput {\n  padding: 0.5rem;\n  width: 200px;\n}\n\nbutton {\n  padding: 0.5rem 1rem;\n  cursor: pointer;\n  background-color: #000000;\n  color: white;\n}\n\n#statusMessage {\n  margin-top: 1.5rem;\n  font-weight: bold;\n  height: 1.5rem;\n}\n</style>\n<script>\nconst library = [\n  \"The Hobbit\", \"1984\", \"Dune\", \"Fahrenheit 451\", \"To Kill a Mockingbird\",\n  \"The Great Gatsby\", \"Moby Dick\", \"Pride and Prejudice\", \"Frankenstein\"\n];\n\nconst searchBtn = document.getElementById(\"search\");\nconst statusDisplay = document.getElementById(\"statusMessage\");\nconst bookInput = document.getElementById(\"bookTitleInput\");\n\nconst findBook = (title) => {\n  \n};\n\nsearchBtn.addEventListener(\"click\", () => {\n  const result = findBook(bookInput.value.trim());\n  statusDisplay.textContent = result;\n});\n</script>","seedLang":"html","region":[56,57],"solution":null,"solutionLang":null,"beforeAll":"","beforeEach":""},{"kind":"challenge","id":"6a1c46843b36624be487988d","title":"Step 3","challengeType":0,"demoType":null,"description":"Now you need to handle potential problems. What if the user clicks the \"Search\" button without typing anything into the input field?\n\nRight now, the function takes that empty string and returns `\" is available to borrow!\"`. This is not very helpful.\n\nIn situations like this you can use the `throw` keyword to throw a `new Error()` and halt the function execution. For example:\n\n```js\nthrow new Error(\"Your error message goes here\");\n```\n\nBefore your success `return` statement, add an `if` statement that checks if the `title` parameter is strictly equal to an empty string (`\"\"`).\n\nInside the `if` statement, use the `throw` keyword to throw a `new Error()`.\nPass the exact string `\"Search field cannot be empty\"` into the `Error` constructor.\n\n**Note:** If you click the Search button after writing this, nothing will appear on the screen! The error stops the click handler because nothing is catching it yet. You will fix this in a few steps. However, you can check the console for now to confirm your code works.","hints":[{"text":"You should create an `if` statement that checks if `title` is strictly equal to an empty string, and throw a `new Error()` with the exact string `\"Search field cannot be empty\"` inside it.","test":"assert.throws(() => findBook(\"\"), Error, \"Search field cannot be empty\");"},{"text":"Your `findBook` function should return `\"The Hobbit is available to borrow!\"` when called with `\"The Hobbit\"`.","test":"assert.strictEqual(findBook(\"The Hobbit\"), \"The Hobbit is available to borrow!\");"}],"seed":"<!DOCTYPE html>\n<html lang=\"en\">\n  <head>\n    <meta charset=\"UTF-8\" />\n    <meta name=\"viewport\" content=\"width=device-width, initial-scale=1.0\" />\n    <title>Library Book Search</title>\n    <link rel=\"stylesheet\" href=\"styles.css\" />\n  </head>\n  <body>\n    <div>\n      <h1>Find book title</h1>\n      <input type=\"text\" id=\"bookTitleInput\" placeholder=\"Enter book title\">\n      <button id=\"search\">Search</button>\n      <p id=\"statusMessage\"></p>\n      <p id=\"scanStatus\"></p>\n    </div>\n    <script src=\"script.js\"></script>\n  </body>\n</html>\n<style class=\"fcc-injected-styles\">\nbody {\n  display: flex;\n  justify-content: center;\n  align-items: center;\n  height: 100vh;\n}\n\ninput {\n  padding: 0.5rem;\n  width: 200px;\n}\n\nbutton {\n  padding: 0.5rem 1rem;\n  cursor: pointer;\n  background-color: #000000;\n  color: white;\n}\n\n#statusMessage {\n  margin-top: 1.5rem;\n  font-weight: bold;\n  height: 1.5rem;\n}\n</style>\n<script>\nconst library = [\n  \"The Hobbit\", \"1984\", \"Dune\", \"Fahrenheit 451\", \"To Kill a Mockingbird\",\n  \"The Great Gatsby\", \"Moby Dick\", \"Pride and Prejudice\", \"Frankenstein\"\n];\n\nconst searchBtn = document.getElementById(\"search\");\nconst statusDisplay = document.getElementById(\"statusMessage\");\nconst bookInput = document.getElementById(\"bookTitleInput\");\n\nconst findBook = (title) => {\n  \n  return title + \" is available to borrow!\";\n};\n\nsearchBtn.addEventListener(\"click\", () => {\n  const result = findBook(bookInput.value.trim());\n  statusDisplay.textContent = result;\n});\n</script>","seedLang":"html","region":[56,57],"solution":null,"solutionLang":null,"beforeAll":"","beforeEach":""},{"kind":"challenge","id":"6a1c48b465a40113ecf3766f","title":"Step 4","challengeType":0,"demoType":null,"description":"Your function will now stop running and throw an error if the user tries to search with an empty field.\n\nBut what if the user searches for a book that is not in your `library` array? You need to handle that exception, too.\n\nBelow your first `if` statement, create a second `if` statement. Inside the condition, use the logical NOT operator (`!`) and the `.includes()` method to check if the `library` array does not include the `title` passed into the function.\n\nInside the `if` statement, use the `throw` keyword to throw a `new Error()`. Pass the expression `title + \" not found!\"` into the `Error` constructor.","hints":[{"text":"You should create a second `if` statement that checks if `library` does not include `title`, and throw a `new Error()` with the expression `title + \" not found!\"` inside it.","test":"assert.throws(() => findBook(\"Nonexistent Book\"), Error, \"Nonexistent Book not found!\");"},{"text":"Your `findBook` function should return `\"The Hobbit is available to borrow!\"` when called with `\"The Hobbit\"`.","test":"assert.strictEqual(findBook(\"The Hobbit\"), \"The Hobbit is available to borrow!\");"}],"seed":"<!DOCTYPE html>\n<html lang=\"en\">\n  <head>\n    <meta charset=\"UTF-8\" />\n    <meta name=\"viewport\" content=\"width=device-width, initial-scale=1.0\" />\n    <title>Library Book Search</title>\n    <link rel=\"stylesheet\" href=\"styles.css\" />\n  </head>\n  <body>\n    <div>\n      <h1>Find book title</h1>\n      <input type=\"text\" id=\"bookTitleInput\" placeholder=\"Enter book title\">\n      <button id=\"search\">Search</button>\n      <p id=\"statusMessage\"></p>\n      <p id=\"scanStatus\"></p>\n    </div>\n    <script src=\"script.js\"></script>\n  </body>\n</html>\n<style class=\"fcc-injected-styles\">\nbody {\n  display: flex;\n  justify-content: center;\n  align-items: center;\n  height: 100vh;\n}\n\ninput {\n  padding: 0.5rem;\n  width: 200px;\n}\n\nbutton {\n  padding: 0.5rem 1rem;\n  cursor: pointer;\n  background-color: #000000;\n  color: white;\n}\n\n#statusMessage {\n  margin-top: 1.5rem;\n  font-weight: bold;\n  height: 1.5rem;\n}\n</style>\n<script>\nconst library = [\n  \"The Hobbit\", \"1984\", \"Dune\", \"Fahrenheit 451\", \"To Kill a Mockingbird\",\n  \"The Great Gatsby\", \"Moby Dick\", \"Pride and Prejudice\", \"Frankenstein\"\n];\n\nconst searchBtn = document.getElementById(\"search\");\nconst statusDisplay = document.getElementById(\"statusMessage\");\nconst bookInput = document.getElementById(\"bookTitleInput\");\n\nconst findBook = (title) => {\n  if (title === \"\") {\n    throw new Error(\"Search field cannot be empty\");\n  }\n\n  \n\n  return title + \" is available to borrow!\";\n};\n\nsearchBtn.addEventListener(\"click\", () => {\n  const result = findBook(bookInput.value.trim());\n  statusDisplay.textContent = result;\n});\n</script>","seedLang":"html","region":[60,61],"solution":null,"solutionLang":null,"beforeAll":"","beforeEach":""},{"kind":"challenge","id":"6a1c49231334592cc5a4c6b0","title":"Step 5","challengeType":0,"demoType":null,"description":"Great! However, if your `findBook` function throws an error, the rest of the code in your event listener won't run because nothing is there to catch it.\n\nTo handle these errors gracefully, you need to wrap the code that might fail inside a `try` block.\n\n```js\ntry {\n  // Code that might throw an error\n}\n```\n\nLook at the `searchBtn` event listener at the bottom of the file. Wrap the existing code in that callback function in a `try` block.\n\n**Note:** Ignore the red error line below the curly braces. You will fix that in the next step.","hints":[{"text":"You should add a `try` block.","test":"assert.match(__helpers.removeJSComments(code), /try\\s*\\{/);"},{"text":"Your `try` block should wrap the `const result` declaration and the `statusDisplay.textContent` assignment.","test":"assert.match(__helpers.removeWhiteSpace(__helpers.removeJSComments(code)), /try\\{constresult=findBook\\(bookInput\\.value\\.trim\\(\\)\\);?statusDisplay\\.textContent=result;?\\}/);"}],"seed":"<!DOCTYPE html>\n<html lang=\"en\">\n  <head>\n    <meta charset=\"UTF-8\" />\n    <meta name=\"viewport\" content=\"width=device-width, initial-scale=1.0\" />\n    <title>Library Book Search</title>\n    <link rel=\"stylesheet\" href=\"styles.css\" />\n  </head>\n  <body>\n    <div>\n      <h1>Find book title</h1>\n      <input type=\"text\" id=\"bookTitleInput\" placeholder=\"Enter book title\">\n      <button id=\"search\">Search</button>\n      <p id=\"statusMessage\"></p>\n      <p id=\"scanStatus\"></p>\n    </div>\n    <script src=\"script.js\"></script>\n  </body>\n</html>\n<style class=\"fcc-injected-styles\">\nbody {\n  display: flex;\n  justify-content: center;\n  align-items: center;\n  height: 100vh;\n}\n\ninput {\n  padding: 0.5rem;\n  width: 200px;\n}\n\nbutton {\n  padding: 0.5rem 1rem;\n  cursor: pointer;\n  background-color: #000000;\n  color: white;\n}\n\n#statusMessage {\n  margin-top: 1.5rem;\n  font-weight: bold;\n  height: 1.5rem;\n}\n</style>\n<script>\nconst library = [\n  \"The Hobbit\", \"1984\", \"Dune\", \"Fahrenheit 451\", \"To Kill a Mockingbird\",\n  \"The Great Gatsby\", \"Moby Dick\", \"Pride and Prejudice\", \"Frankenstein\"\n];\n\nconst searchBtn = document.getElementById(\"search\");\nconst statusDisplay = document.getElementById(\"statusMessage\");\nconst bookInput = document.getElementById(\"bookTitleInput\");\n\nconst findBook = (title) => {\n  if (title === \"\") {\n    throw new Error(\"Search field cannot be empty\");\n  }\n\n  if (!library.includes(title)) {\n    throw new Error(title + \" not found!\");\n  }\n\n  return title + \" is available to borrow!\";\n};\n\nsearchBtn.addEventListener(\"click\", () => {\n  const result = findBook(bookInput.value.trim());\n  statusDisplay.textContent = result;\n});\n</script>","seedLang":"html","region":[68,70],"solution":null,"solutionLang":null,"beforeAll":"","beforeEach":""},{"kind":"challenge","id":"6a1c4995ca0a594713d129e1","title":"Step 6","challengeType":0,"demoType":null,"description":"To capture and handle the errors from your `try` block, you need to add a `catch` block. You can declare a parameter for this block to inspect the actual error object that was thrown.\n\n```js\ntry {\n  // Code that might throw an error\n} catch (err) {\n  // Code to handle the error\n}\n```\n\nDirectly below your `try` block, add a `catch` block with a parameter named `err`. Inside the block, update the DOM by setting the `textContent` of `statusDisplay` to `err.message`.","hints":[{"text":"You should add a `catch` block with `err` as a parameter. Inside the block, you should set `statusDisplay.textContent` to `err.message`.","test":"document.getElementById('bookTitleInput').value = \"Nonexistent Book\";\ndocument.getElementById('search').click();\nassert.strictEqual(document.getElementById('statusMessage').textContent, \"Nonexistent Book not found!\");\n\ndocument.getElementById('bookTitleInput').value = \"\";\ndocument.getElementById('search').click();\nassert.strictEqual(document.getElementById('statusMessage').textContent, \"Search field cannot be empty\");"}],"seed":"<!DOCTYPE html>\n<html lang=\"en\">\n  <head>\n    <meta charset=\"UTF-8\" />\n    <meta name=\"viewport\" content=\"width=device-width, initial-scale=1.0\" />\n    <title>Library Book Search</title>\n    <link rel=\"stylesheet\" href=\"styles.css\" />\n  </head>\n  <body>\n    <div>\n      <h1>Find book title</h1>\n      <input type=\"text\" id=\"bookTitleInput\" placeholder=\"Enter book title\">\n      <button id=\"search\">Search</button>\n      <p id=\"statusMessage\"></p>\n      <p id=\"scanStatus\"></p>\n    </div>\n    <script src=\"script.js\"></script>\n  </body>\n</html>\n<style class=\"fcc-injected-styles\">\nbody {\n  display: flex;\n  justify-content: center;\n  align-items: center;\n  height: 100vh;\n}\n\ninput {\n  padding: 0.5rem;\n  width: 200px;\n}\n\nbutton {\n  padding: 0.5rem 1rem;\n  cursor: pointer;\n  background-color: #000000;\n  color: white;\n}\n\n#statusMessage {\n  margin-top: 1.5rem;\n  font-weight: bold;\n  height: 1.5rem;\n}\n</style>\n<script>\nconst library = [\n  \"The Hobbit\", \"1984\", \"Dune\", \"Fahrenheit 451\", \"To Kill a Mockingbird\",\n  \"The Great Gatsby\", \"Moby Dick\", \"Pride and Prejudice\", \"Frankenstein\"\n];\n\nconst searchBtn = document.getElementById(\"search\");\nconst statusDisplay = document.getElementById(\"statusMessage\");\nconst bookInput = document.getElementById(\"bookTitleInput\");\n\nconst findBook = (title) => {\n  if (title === \"\") {\n    throw new Error(\"Search field cannot be empty\");\n  }\n\n  if (!library.includes(title)) {\n    throw new Error(title + \" not found!\");\n  }\n\n  return title + \" is available to borrow!\";\n};\n\nsearchBtn.addEventListener(\"click\", () => {\n  try {\n    const result = findBook(bookInput.value.trim());\n    statusDisplay.textContent = result;\n  }\n});\n</script>","seedLang":"html","region":[71,72],"solution":null,"solutionLang":null,"beforeAll":"","beforeEach":""},{"kind":"challenge","id":"6a1c49deb109227f4020e0e9","title":"Step 7","challengeType":0,"demoType":null,"description":"Cool! But as a developer, you also need to see the full error object to debug effectively.\n\n```js\nconsole.error(err);\n```\n\nInside your `catch` block, right below where you update the `textContent`, add a `console.error()` statement and pass it your `err` object.","hints":[{"text":"You should call the `console.error()` method inside your `catch` block.","test":"const spy = __helpers.spyOn(console, 'error');\ntry {\n  document.getElementById('bookTitleInput').value = \"Nonexistent Book\";\n  document.getElementById('search').click();\n  assert.isAtLeast(spy.calls.length, 1);\n  assert.instanceOf(spy.calls[0][0], Error);\n  assert.strictEqual(spy.calls[0][0].message, \"Nonexistent Book not found!\");\n} finally {\n  spy.restore();\n}"}],"seed":"<!DOCTYPE html>\n<html lang=\"en\">\n  <head>\n    <meta charset=\"UTF-8\" />\n    <meta name=\"viewport\" content=\"width=device-width, initial-scale=1.0\" />\n    <title>Library Book Search</title>\n    <link rel=\"stylesheet\" href=\"styles.css\" />\n  </head>\n  <body>\n    <div>\n      <h1>Find book title</h1>\n      <input type=\"text\" id=\"bookTitleInput\" placeholder=\"Enter book title\">\n      <button id=\"search\">Search</button>\n      <p id=\"statusMessage\"></p>\n      <p id=\"scanStatus\"></p>\n    </div>\n    <script src=\"script.js\"></script>\n  </body>\n</html>\n<style class=\"fcc-injected-styles\">\nbody {\n  display: flex;\n  justify-content: center;\n  align-items: center;\n  height: 100vh;\n}\n\ninput {\n  padding: 0.5rem;\n  width: 200px;\n}\n\nbutton {\n  padding: 0.5rem 1rem;\n  cursor: pointer;\n  background-color: #000000;\n  color: white;\n}\n\n#statusMessage {\n  margin-top: 1.5rem;\n  font-weight: bold;\n  height: 1.5rem;\n}\n</style>\n<script>\nconst library = [\n  \"The Hobbit\", \"1984\", \"Dune\", \"Fahrenheit 451\", \"To Kill a Mockingbird\",\n  \"The Great Gatsby\", \"Moby Dick\", \"Pride and Prejudice\", \"Frankenstein\"\n];\n\nconst searchBtn = document.getElementById(\"search\");\nconst statusDisplay = document.getElementById(\"statusMessage\");\nconst bookInput = document.getElementById(\"bookTitleInput\");\n\nconst findBook = (title) => {\n  if (title === \"\") {\n    throw new Error(\"Search field cannot be empty\");\n  }\n\n  if (!library.includes(title)) {\n    throw new Error(title + \" not found!\");\n  }\n\n  return title + \" is available to borrow!\";\n};\n\nsearchBtn.addEventListener(\"click\", () => {\n  try {\n    const result = findBook(bookInput.value.trim());\n    statusDisplay.textContent = result;\n  } catch (err) {\n    statusDisplay.textContent = err.message;\n    \n  }\n});\n</script>","seedLang":"html","region":[73,74],"solution":null,"solutionLang":null,"beforeAll":"","beforeEach":""},{"kind":"challenge","id":"6a1c4a2773f60894ce905694","title":"Step 8","challengeType":0,"demoType":null,"description":"For advanced debugging, you can use the `debugger` statement to pause your JavaScript execution and inspect your program.\n\nInside your `catch` block, right below your `console.error()` statement, add a `debugger;` statement.\n\n**Note:** With your browser's Developer Tools open, search for an unavailable book or leave the input empty and click Search. The resulting error will enter the `catch` block, and execution will pause at the `debugger;` statement.","hints":[{"text":"You should add a `debugger;` statement inside your `catch` block.","test":"assert.match(__helpers.removeWhiteSpace(__helpers.removeJSComments(code)), /console\\.error\\(err\\);debugger[;}]/);"}],"seed":"<!DOCTYPE html>\n<html lang=\"en\">\n  <head>\n    <meta charset=\"UTF-8\" />\n    <meta name=\"viewport\" content=\"width=device-width, initial-scale=1.0\" />\n    <title>Library Book Search</title>\n    <link rel=\"stylesheet\" href=\"styles.css\" />\n  </head>\n  <body>\n    <div>\n      <h1>Find book title</h1>\n      <input type=\"text\" id=\"bookTitleInput\" placeholder=\"Enter book title\">\n      <button id=\"search\">Search</button>\n      <p id=\"statusMessage\"></p>\n      <p id=\"scanStatus\"></p>\n    </div>\n    <script src=\"script.js\"></script>\n  </body>\n</html>\n<style class=\"fcc-injected-styles\">\nbody {\n  display: flex;\n  justify-content: center;\n  align-items: center;\n  height: 100vh;\n}\n\ninput {\n  padding: 0.5rem;\n  width: 200px;\n}\n\nbutton {\n  padding: 0.5rem 1rem;\n  cursor: pointer;\n  background-color: #000000;\n  color: white;\n}\n\n#statusMessage {\n  margin-top: 1.5rem;\n  font-weight: bold;\n  height: 1.5rem;\n}\n</style>\n<script>\nconst library = [\n  \"The Hobbit\", \"1984\", \"Dune\", \"Fahrenheit 451\", \"To Kill a Mockingbird\",\n  \"The Great Gatsby\", \"Moby Dick\", \"Pride and Prejudice\", \"Frankenstein\"\n];\n\nconst searchBtn = document.getElementById(\"search\");\nconst statusDisplay = document.getElementById(\"statusMessage\");\nconst bookInput = document.getElementById(\"bookTitleInput\");\n\nconst findBook = (title) => {\n  if (title === \"\") {\n    throw new Error(\"Search field cannot be empty\");\n  }\n\n  if (!library.includes(title)) {\n    throw new Error(title + \" not found!\");\n  }\n\n  return title + \" is available to borrow!\";\n};\n\nsearchBtn.addEventListener(\"click\", () => {\n  try {\n    const result = findBook(bookInput.value.trim());\n    statusDisplay.textContent = result;\n  } catch (err) {\n    statusDisplay.textContent = err.message;\n    console.error(err);\n    \n  }\n});\n</script>","seedLang":"html","region":[74,75],"solution":null,"solutionLang":null,"beforeAll":"","beforeEach":""},{"kind":"challenge","id":"6a1c4a5b9ecacf56d79ee405","title":"Step 9","challengeType":0,"demoType":null,"description":"You have successfully handled both the success and error states. But what if there is code you want to run regardless of what happens? That is where the `finally` block comes in.\n\n```js\ntry {\n  // Code that might throw an error\n} catch (err) {\n  // Code to handle the error\n} finally {\n  // Code to run regardless of success or failure\n}\n```\n\nDirectly below your `catch` block, add a `finally` block. Inside it, set the `value` property of `bookInput` to an empty string (`\"\"`) to clear the input field so the user can immediately type a new search.","hints":[{"text":"You should add a `finally` block directly after your `catch` block, and set `bookInput.value` to an empty string inside it.","test":"assert.match(__helpers.removeJSComments(code), /\\}\\s*finally\\s*\\{/);\n\ndocument.getElementById('bookTitleInput').value = \"The Hobbit\";\ndocument.getElementById('search').click();\nassert.strictEqual(document.getElementById('bookTitleInput').value, \"\");\n\ndocument.getElementById('bookTitleInput').value = \"Test Search\";\ndocument.getElementById('search').click();\nassert.strictEqual(document.getElementById('bookTitleInput').value, \"\");"}],"seed":"<!DOCTYPE html>\n<html lang=\"en\">\n  <head>\n    <meta charset=\"UTF-8\" />\n    <meta name=\"viewport\" content=\"width=device-width, initial-scale=1.0\" />\n    <title>Library Book Search</title>\n    <link rel=\"stylesheet\" href=\"styles.css\" />\n  </head>\n  <body>\n    <div>\n      <h1>Find book title</h1>\n      <input type=\"text\" id=\"bookTitleInput\" placeholder=\"Enter book title\">\n      <button id=\"search\">Search</button>\n      <p id=\"statusMessage\"></p>\n      <p id=\"scanStatus\"></p>\n    </div>\n    <script src=\"script.js\"></script>\n  </body>\n</html>\n<style class=\"fcc-injected-styles\">\nbody {\n  display: flex;\n  justify-content: center;\n  align-items: center;\n  height: 100vh;\n}\n\ninput {\n  padding: 0.5rem;\n  width: 200px;\n}\n\nbutton {\n  padding: 0.5rem 1rem;\n  cursor: pointer;\n  background-color: #000000;\n  color